  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-adaptive-simpson-refinement-indicator": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $a<b$, let $n\\ge2$ be even, and let $f$ be real-valued at the nodes of\nthe uniform grids with $n$ and $2n$ subintervals. The **coarse Simpson value**\nis $S_0=Q_S(f;a,b,n)$ and the **refined value** is\n$S_1=Q_S(f;a,b,2n)$, using [[alg-composite-simpson-rule]]. The signed\n**refinement indicator** is $(S_1-S_0)/15$ and its magnitude is\n$$\\eta_n(f;a,b):=\\frac{|S_1-S_0|}{15}.$$\n\nFor the local two-panel case $n=2$, $S_1$ is also the sum of the two\none-block Simpson values on the halves of $[a,b]$.\n\nThis definition alone is not a bound on the true error."
    ],
    "lem-composite-simpson-asymptotic-error-expansion": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $a<b$, let $k\\ge1$ be an integer, set $n_k=2^k$ and\n$h_k=(b-a)/n_k$, and let $Q_k=Q_S(f;a,b,n_k)$ for $f\\in C^4([a,b])$. With\n$I=\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx$, one has\n$$I-Q_k=-\\frac{h_k^4}{180}\\int_a^b f^{(4)}(x)\\,dx+o(h_k^4)\\qquad(k\\to\\infty).$$\nHere $r_k=o(h_k^4)$ means $r_k/h_k^4\\to0$ along this sequence. The\nintegral is the Riemann integral."
    ],
    "alg-adaptive-simpson-with-finite-depth-and-panel-cap": [
      "## Definition\n\nInput $a<b$, an absolute indicator tolerance $\\tau>0$, a remaining depth\n$D\\in\\mathbb N$ and a positive panel-call budget $P\\in\\mathbb N$, and a\ndeterministic evaluation routine $\\operatorname{Eval}$ that returns a finite\nreal value at a requested node or reports failure. It reports failure when a\nrequired node cannot be evaluated, including when its midpoint is\nunrepresentable in the evaluator's arithmetic. Apply the exact-real Simpson\nformulas of [[alg-composite-simpson-rule]] to the returned values;\nfloating-point rounding accuracy is not asserted.\n\nDefine the recursive call $A(u,v,d,p,t)$ for $u<v$, $d\\ge0$, $p\\ge1$ and\n$t>0$ as follows.\n\n1. Compute $m=(u+v)/2$ and the five values of $\\operatorname{Eval}$ at\n   $u,(3u+v)/4,m,(u+3v)/4,v$. On failure, return `failure` with no estimate.\n2. Compute $S_0=Q_S(f;u,v,2)$ and $S_1=Q_S(f;u,v,4)$ from the two nested\n   grids of [[def-adaptive-simpson-refinement-indicator]], and put\n   $\\eta=\\eta_2(f;u,v)=|S_1-S_0|/15$.\n3. If $\\eta\\le t$, return (`accepted`, estimate $S_1$, indicator $\\eta$).\n4. If $\\eta>t$ and either $d=0$ or $p<3$, return (`cap-reached`, estimate\n   $S_1$, indicator $\\eta$).\n5. Otherwise put $p_L=\\lfloor(p-1)/2\\rfloor$ and\n   $p_R=\\lceil(p-1)/2\\rceil$. Recursively call\n   $A(u,m,d-1,p_L,t/2)$ and $A(m,v,d-1,p_R,t/2)$. If either returns failure,\n   return failure. Otherwise return the sum of their estimates and indicators,\n   with status `cap-reached` if either child has that status and `accepted`\n   otherwise.\n\nThe public call is $A(a,b,D,P,\\tau)$. The budget counts recursive panel calls;\neach call uses at most five point evaluations. `accepted` means that the\nreturned leaf indicators sum to at most $\\tau$. Neither that status nor the\nindicator is a certified true-error statement. A cap result is an estimate\nwith explicit `cap-reached` status, not a successful tolerance claim."
    ],
    "thm-simpson-richardson-error-estimator-asymptotic": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $a<b$, $f\\in C^4([a,b])$, $n_k=2^k$ for $k\\ge1$,\n$h_k=(b-a)/n_k$, $Q_k=Q_S(f;a,b,n_k)$, and\n$c=-(1/180)\\int_a^b f^{(4)}(x)\\,dx$. Let $I=\\int_a^b f$. Then the\nrefinement indicator\n$$\\eta_k=\\frac{|Q_{k+1}-Q_k|}{15}$$\nsatisfies $\\eta_k=|c|h_k^4/16+o(h_k^4)$. If $c\\ne0$, then\n$\\eta_k/|I-Q_{k+1}|\\to1$. The Richardson-corrected value\n$R_{k+1}=Q_{k+1}+(Q_{k+1}-Q_k)/15$ satisfies\n$I-R_{k+1}=o(h_k^4)$. When $c=0$, these statements give no relative\nerror estimate for $\\eta_k$; at no finite level does $\\eta_k$ alone certify\nthe true error."
    ],
    "thm-adaptive-simpson-cap-termination-and-indicator-budget": [
      "## Statement\n\nFor valid inputs, [[alg-adaptive-simpson-with-finite-depth-and-panel-cap]]\nterminates after at most $P$ recursive panel calls and at most $5P$ evaluation\nrequests. A finite result is a sum of the refined Simpson values on the\nreturned leaf intervals, and its reported indicator is the sum of their\nnonnegative indicators. An evaluation failure, including an unrepresentable\nrequired node reported by the evaluator, returns `failure` with no estimate.\nIf the status is `accepted`, that indicator sum is at\nmost the requested tolerance $\\tau$. If a rejected leaf cannot split because\nof the depth or panel-call cap, the result has status `cap-reached`. These\nclaims do not bound the true quadrature error."
    ],
    "alg-certified-adaptive-simpson": [
      "## Definition\n\nInput finite dyadic endpoints $a<b$, a positive dyadic tolerance $\\tau$, integers $D\\ge0$ and\n$P\\ge1$, and a function $f\\in C^4([a,b])$. Also supply two validated\nenclosure routines:\n\n- $V(x)$ returns a finite real interval containing the exact value $f(x)$;\n- $Y([u,v])$ returns a finite real interval containing every\n  $f^{(4)}(x)$ for $x\\in[u,v]$.\n\nIf either routine does not meet its enclosure contract, this algorithm makes\nno certification claim; soundness is a mathematical caller precondition and\ncannot be detected from samples. An explicit oracle failure or malformed or\nnon-finite interval result returns `failure`. The intervals and operations are those of\n[[def-intervals-rectangles-and-complex-balls]] and\n[[def-outward-rounded-interval-and-ball-operations]]. The function\n$\\operatorname{ru}$ is upward directed rounding from\n[[def-dyadic-floating-point-format-and-directed-rounding]]. A midpoint that is not\nexactly representable or a non-finite interval operation returns `failure`\nwith no enclosure; malformed or non-finite oracle results are covered by the\nexplicit failure rule above.\n\nDefine $A([u,v],d,p,t)$ recursively. Form the exact midpoint $m=(u+v)/2$; if\nit is not representable in the format, return `failure`. Query $V(u),V(m),V(v)$\nand let $Y([u,v])=[y_-,y_+]$. Put $M=\\max(|y_-|,|y_+|)$ and let $L=v-u$\nbe the exact difference of the dyadic endpoints. Form an outward-rounded\ninterval $W=[v,v]-[u,u]$ by interval subtraction, and compute\n$S_P:=(W/[6,6])\\bigl(V(u)+4V(m)+V(v)\\bigr)$ using outward interval\noperations. Compute $B_P=\\operatorname{ru}(ML^5/2880)$ by upward rounding the\nexact real expression; if it overflows, return `failure`. Set\n$C_P=S_P+[-B_P,B_P]$ by outward interval addition. If any interval operation\nhas a non-finite status, return `failure`. Treat the endpoints of $C_P$ as exact\ndyadic rationals and report their exact rational midpoint $q_P$ and half-width\n$r_P$; compare $r_P$ with $t$ exactly.\n\n1. If $r_P\\le t$, return (`certified`, enclosure $C_P$, estimate $q_P$,\n   error bound $r_P$).\n2. If $r_P>t$ and either $d=0$ or $p<3$, return\n   (`inconclusive`, enclosure $C_P$, estimate $q_P$, error bound $r_P$).\n3. Otherwise split at $m$, allocate\n   $p_L=\\lfloor(p-1)/2\\rfloor$ and $p_R=\\lceil(p-1)/2\\rceil$, and recurse\n   with depth $d-1$ and tolerance $t/2$ on each half. A failed child or\n   non-finite interval sum returns `failure`. Otherwise outward-add the child\n   enclosures to obtain $C$, with exact midpoint $q$ and half-width $r$;\n   return `certified` if $r\\le t$, and `inconclusive` otherwise.\n\nThe public call is $A([a,b],D,P,\\tau)$. `certified` asserts an error at most\n$r\\le\\tau$; `inconclusive` still returns a valid enclosure and bound but does\nnot assert the requested tolerance was met. The derivative enclosure routine\nis an explicit mathematical precondition, not something inferred from\nsampled values or the Simpson indicator. Outward rounding of all interval\noperations, and the separate Peano remainder bound, are essential."
    ],
    "thm-certified-adaptive-simpson-enclosure": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the input hypotheses and the two sound enclosure-oracle contracts in\n[[alg-certified-adaptive-simpson]]. Whenever the algorithm returns `certified`\nor `inconclusive` with a finite enclosure $C=[L,U]$, the exact Riemann\nintegral $I=\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx$ belongs to $C$. Its reported center\n$q=(L+U)/2$ and half-width $r=(U-L)/2$ satisfy $|I-q|\\le r$. A `certified`\nstatus additionally means $r\\le\\tau$. The algorithm terminates after at\nmost $P$ recursive panel calls. If an oracle reports failure or returns a\nmalformed or non-finite interval, or an interval operation is non-finite, the\nalgorithm returns `failure` and asserts no finite enclosure. An `inconclusive` result\ndoes not assert that the requested tolerance was met."
    ],
